{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016hQsNJeeFHAsnCpgLcasRy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01G3NX7j17XMN1tmZ9ZwYabH","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. Review the following paper thoroughly from those angles.\n\n**Paper**: \"Diagnostic Analysis of Value Overestimation from Temporal Abstraction in DreamerV3\"\n\n## Paper Summary (from PDF, 12 pages)\n\nThe paper identifies Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO) as a fundamental failure mode when integrating variable-rate temporal abstraction into DreamerV3's imagination-based actor-critic. Key contributions:\n\n1. **CIVO causal chain**: A trained temporal embedding learns dt-specific representations on RSSM states whose fidelity degrades at dt>1 (reward prediction gap 2.78×). The critic bootstraps from these imprecise-but-confidently-conditioned states → pathological value overestimation (NVE≥1.5× in 9/9 trained seeds, 0/12 untrained; AUROC=0.969). A 2×2 ablation matrix (trained/frozen × exposed/unexposed) confirms both embedding training AND exposure to dt>1 data are jointly necessary.\n\n2. **MPC vs. imagination architectural boundary**: dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC (TAWM) but fails in imagination-based AC. Critic bootstrapping from imagined states is the vulnerability — MPC re-plans from real states.\n\n3. **Intervention Hierarchy**: 8 methods × 3 levels (gradient, output, representation) × 3 environments × 42 seeds. No post-hoc intervention reliably eliminates CIVO. Key insight: gradient magnitude ≠ causal importance (reward: 38-49% of gradients but causally irrelevant; obs-decoder: 16-22% but causally critical).\n\n## Methodology Details\n\n- **Variable-rate imagination**: Extends DreamerV3 rollouts with temporal strides dt∈{1,2,4}. Learnable temporal embedding e_dt ∈ R^d concatenated to RSSM input. Optional subsampling (skip every other transition when dt=2).\n- **Factorial design**: 2 binary factors — embedding state (trained/frozen/removed) × data exposure (subsample ON/OFF).\n- **NVE metric**: NVE(t) = [V_exp(t)/R_exp(t)] / [V_base(t)/R_base(t)]. NVE≈1.0 healthy; NVE≫1.0 pathological. Threshold 1.5×.\n- **Environments**: Walker Walk (24D, primary), Cheetah Run (17D), Cartpole Swingup (5D), Hopper Hop (15D). All proprioceptive.\n\n## Key Experimental Tables\n\n**Table 1 (RSSM Fidelity)**:\n| Model | dt1 obs MSE | dt2 obs MSE | Obs gap | dt1 rew MAE | dt2 rew MAE | Rew gap |\n|-------|-------------|-------------|---------|-------------|-------------|---------|\n| Baseline (dt=1) | 13.49 | 27.50 | 2.04× | 0.010 | 0.016 | 1.56× |\n| 008d (trained emb) | 41.31 | 53.67 | 1.30×* | 0.057 | 0.158 | 2.78× |\n| 008f (no emb) | 8.01 | 22.04 | 2.75× | 0.747 | 0.753 | 1.01× |\n*Misleadingly low: dt1 absolute error 3× baseline\n\n**Table 2 (Walker 008d cross-seed)**:\n| Seed | Eval@105K | NVE peak | Peak step | Collapse? | Pattern |\n|------|-----------|----------|-----------|-----------|---------|\n| s0 | 82.75 | 4.12 | 55K | Yes | Value-driven, entropy ↓ |\n| s1 | 131.08 | 4.12 | 55K | Yes | Deterministic replication |\n| s2 | 267.93 | 2.05 | 25K | No | Transient → self-correction |\n\n**Table 3 (2×2 Ablation)**:\n| Config | Emb state | Exposed to dt>1? | CIVO? | Eval@105K | NVE range |\n|--------|-----------|------------------|-------|-----------|-----------|\n| 008d | Trained | Yes | Yes (2/2) | 82-131 | 1.71-4.12 |\n| 008a | Trained | No | No (0/3) | 631-899 | 0.51-0.65 |\n| 008f | Removed | Yes | No (0/4) | 148-503 | 0.30-1.41 |\n| 015b | Frozen | Yes | No (0/1) | 204 | 0.39-0.47 |\n\n**Table 6 (NVE Threshold AUROC)**:\n| Threshold | Sensitivity | Specificity | Accuracy | Youden J |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|----------|----------|\n| 1.0× | 1.00 | 0.72 | 0.83 | 0.72 |\n| 1.5× | **0.93** | **0.97** | **0.95** | **0.90** |\n| 2.0× | 0.75 | 1.00 | 0.88 | 0.75 |\nAUROC=0.969, Youden-optimal=1.53×\n\n**Table 7 (Cross-Environment NVE)**:\n| Environment | Trained NVE>1.5× | Untrained NVE>1.5× | Max NVE |\n|-------------|-------------------|---------------------|---------|\n| Walker Walk | 5/5 | 0/7 | 4.12× |\n| Cheetah Run | 3/3 | 0/5 | 70.64× |\n| PickCube | 1/1 | — | 3.78× |\n| Total | 9/9 | 0/12 | — |\n\n**Table 8 (Cheetah Run)**: 3/3 collapse for trained emb (NVE peaks 8-70×); 0/3 for no emb; 0/3 for unexposed. Some seeds marked as \"predicted\".\n\n**Tables 9-10 (Cartpole Swingup, Hopper Hop)**: Marked as \"predicted\" based on observed patterns.\n\n**Table 11 (dt=1 imagination, 008e_v3)**: 2/4 collapse (50%), same as 008d. Identifies Pathway A (value-driven) vs Pathway B (eval-driven).\n\n**Table 12 (Pathway A vs B)**:\n| Feature | Pathway A (008d) | Pathway B (008e_v3 s2) |\n|---------|------------------|------------------------|\n| Value change | Explosive (+130%) | Moderate (±13%) |\n| Eval change | Post-value (-43%) | Leads (-81%) |\n| Actor entropy | ↓ (policy locks) | ↑ (policy destabilizes) |\n\n**Table 13-15**: Actor entropy signatures, V(s) vs MC returns, embedding cosine similarity.\n\n**Table 16 (Intervention Hierarchy)**:\n| Level | Method | Success rate | Best eval | Failure mechanism |\n|-------|--------|-------------|-----------|-------------------|\n| L1 Gradient | Full SGS | 0/3 | 49% | Forward-pass damage irreducible |\n| L1 Gradient | Obs-decoder SGS | 1/6 | 79%* | Compensatory amplification |\n| L1 Gradient | Reward SGS | 0/2 | 36% | No-op (gradient ≠ causality) |\n| L2 Output | RAVE ceil=2.0 | 0/1 | 3% | EMA anchor pollution |\n| L2 Output | RAVE ceil=1.5 | 0/1 | 8% | EMA anchor pollution |\n| L3 Repr. | TCN σ=0.2 | 2/8 | 91%/45% | Dose insufficient in complex envs |\n| L3 Repr. | TCN σ=0.5 | 0/3 | 40% | Overdose/underdose |\n| L3 Repr. | TCN σ=1.0 | 1/1† | 27% | Learning destroyed by noise |\n| Control | Emb removal | 8/8 | 15-37% | (removes feature entirely) |\n\n## Experiment Coverage Context\n\nThe full experiment design (from review_context.yaml) includes these planned experiments:\n- Walker Walk: 008d multi-seed ✓, 008f multi-seed ✓, 008a multi-seed ✓, 008e_v3 (4 seeds) ✓, 015b frozen ✓\n- Cheetah Run: baseline (5 seeds) ✓, CIVO trigger (2 real + 1 predicted), causal control (2 real + 1 predicted), unexposed (2 real + 1 predicted)\n- Cartpole Swingup: baseline, CIVO trigger, causal control — ALL predicted\n- Hopper Hop: baseline, CIVO trigger, no dt_emb — ALL predicted\n- RSSM fidelity analysis ✓, Actor entropy ✓, V(s) vs MC ✓, Embedding cosine similarity ✓\n- NVE threshold analysis ✓\n- Intervention Hierarchy ✓ (mostly real data)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the experimental design rigorous? Are controls adequate? Is the NVE metric well-defined and justified? Is the 2×2 ablation matrix well-constructed?\n\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are seed counts sufficient? Is reporting of individual seeds (vs. aggregates) appropriate? How robust are claims given the variance?\n\n3. **Experimental completeness**: Cross-reference with the planned experiments above — what fraction is real vs. predicted? Is this disclosed transparently?\n\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters specified?\n\n5. **Claim-evidence alignment**: Does each claim have adequate experimental support? Any overclaiming?\n\n6. **Technical correctness**: Any methodological flaws, confounds, or alternative explanations not considered?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n\n### Summary\n2-3 sentences on what the paper does.\n\n### Strengths (S1-Sn)\nNumbered, specific.\n\n### Weaknesses (W1-Wn)\nNumbered, specific. For each, indicate severity: Minor / Moderate / Major.\n\n### Questions for Authors (Q1-Qn)\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n### Preliminary Score (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\nScore + 1-sentence justification.\n\n### Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":39170,"cache_read_input_tokens":23066,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":39170},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c17d4e9b-d260-4cdf-87d4-b48964bed707","uuid":"81f296fb-2e10-41e1-bc21-276ffb704423"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ae3a708bd00d9db27","tool_use_id":"toolu_01G3NX7j17XMN1tmZ9ZwYabH","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. Review the following paper thoroughly from those angles.\n\n**Paper**: \"Diagnostic Analysis of Value Overestimation from Temporal Abstraction in DreamerV3\"\n\n## Paper Summary (from PDF, 12 pages)\n\nThe paper identifies Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO) as a fundamental failure mode when integrating variable-rate temporal abstraction into DreamerV3's imagination-based actor-critic. Key contributions:\n\n1. **CIVO causal chain**: A trained temporal embedding learns dt-specific representations on RSSM states whose fidelity degrades at dt>1 (reward prediction gap 2.78×). The critic bootstraps from these imprecise-but-confidently-conditioned states → pathological value overestimation (NVE≥1.5× in 9/9 trained seeds, 0/12 untrained; AUROC=0.969). A 2×2 ablation matrix (trained/frozen × exposed/unexposed) confirms both embedding training AND exposure to dt>1 data are jointly necessary.\n\n2. **MPC vs. imagination architectural boundary**: dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC (TAWM) but fails in imagination-based AC. Critic bootstrapping from imagined states is the vulnerability — MPC re-plans from real states.\n\n3. **Intervention Hierarchy**: 8 methods × 3 levels (gradient, output, representation) × 3 environments × 42 seeds. No post-hoc intervention reliably eliminates CIVO. Key insight: gradient magnitude ≠ causal importance (reward: 38-49% of gradients but causally irrelevant; obs-decoder: 16-22% but causally critical).\n\n## Methodology Details\n\n- **Variable-rate imagination**: Extends DreamerV3 rollouts with temporal strides dt∈{1,2,4}. Learnable temporal embedding e_dt ∈ R^d concatenated to RSSM input. Optional subsampling (skip every other transition when dt=2).\n- **Factorial design**: 2 binary factors — embedding state (trained/frozen/removed) × data exposure (subsample ON/OFF).\n- **NVE metric**: NVE(t) = [V_exp(t)/R_exp(t)] / [V_base(t)/R_base(t)]. NVE≈1.0 healthy; NVE≫1.0 pathological. Threshold 1.5×.\n- **Environments**: Walker Walk (24D, primary), Cheetah Run (17D), Cartpole Swingup (5D), Hopper Hop (15D). All proprioceptive.\n\n## Key Experimental Tables\n\n**Table 1 (RSSM Fidelity)**:\n| Model | dt1 obs MSE | dt2 obs MSE | Obs gap | dt1 rew MAE | dt2 rew MAE | Rew gap |\n|-------|-------------|-------------|---------|-------------|-------------|---------|\n| Baseline (dt=1) | 13.49 | 27.50 | 2.04× | 0.010 | 0.016 | 1.56× |\n| 008d (trained emb) | 41.31 | 53.67 | 1.30×* | 0.057 | 0.158 | 2.78× |\n| 008f (no emb) | 8.01 | 22.04 | 2.75× | 0.747 | 0.753 | 1.01× |\n*Misleadingly low: dt1 absolute error 3× baseline\n\n**Table 2 (Walker 008d cross-seed)**:\n| Seed | Eval@105K | NVE peak | Peak step | Collapse? | Pattern |\n|------|-----------|----------|-----------|-----------|---------|\n| s0 | 82.75 | 4.12 | 55K | Yes | Value-driven, entropy ↓ |\n| s1 | 131.08 | 4.12 | 55K | Yes | Deterministic replication |\n| s2 | 267.93 | 2.05 | 25K | No | Transient → self-correction |\n\n**Table 3 (2×2 Ablation)**:\n| Config | Emb state | Exposed to dt>1? | CIVO? | Eval@105K | NVE range |\n|--------|-----------|------------------|-------|-----------|-----------|\n| 008d | Trained | Yes | Yes (2/2) | 82-131 | 1.71-4.12 |\n| 008a | Trained | No | No (0/3) | 631-899 | 0.51-0.65 |\n| 008f | Removed | Yes | No (0/4) | 148-503 | 0.30-1.41 |\n| 015b | Frozen | Yes | No (0/1) | 204 | 0.39-0.47 |\n\n**Table 6 (NVE Threshold AUROC)**:\n| Threshold | Sensitivity | Specificity | Accuracy | Youden J |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|----------|----------|\n| 1.0× | 1.00 | 0.72 | 0.83 | 0.72 |\n| 1.5× | **0.93** | **0.97** | **0.95** | **0.90** |\n| 2.0× | 0.75 | 1.00 | 0.88 | 0.75 |\nAUROC=0.969, Youden-optimal=1.53×\n\n**Table 7 (Cross-Environment NVE)**:\n| Environment | Trained NVE>1.5× | Untrained NVE>1.5× | Max NVE |\n|-------------|-------------------|---------------------|---------|\n| Walker Walk | 5/5 | 0/7 | 4.12× |\n| Cheetah Run | 3/3 | 0/5 | 70.64× |\n| PickCube | 1/1 | — | 3.78× |\n| Total | 9/9 | 0/12 | — |\n\n**Table 8 (Cheetah Run)**: 3/3 collapse for trained emb (NVE peaks 8-70×); 0/3 for no emb; 0/3 for unexposed. Some seeds marked as \"predicted\".\n\n**Tables 9-10 (Cartpole Swingup, Hopper Hop)**: Marked as \"predicted\" based on observed patterns.\n\n**Table 11 (dt=1 imagination, 008e_v3)**: 2/4 collapse (50%), same as 008d. Identifies Pathway A (value-driven) vs Pathway B (eval-driven).\n\n**Table 12 (Pathway A vs B)**:\n| Feature | Pathway A (008d) | Pathway B (008e_v3 s2) |\n|---------|------------------|------------------------|\n| Value change | Explosive (+130%) | Moderate (±13%) |\n| Eval change | Post-value (-43%) | Leads (-81%) |\n| Actor entropy | ↓ (policy locks) | ↑ (policy destabilizes) |\n\n**Table 13-15**: Actor entropy signatures, V(s) vs MC returns, embedding cosine similarity.\n\n**Table 16 (Intervention Hierarchy)**:\n| Level | Method | Success rate | Best eval | Failure mechanism |\n|-------|--------|-------------|-----------|-------------------|\n| L1 Gradient | Full SGS | 0/3 | 49% | Forward-pass damage irreducible |\n| L1 Gradient | Obs-decoder SGS | 1/6 | 79%* | Compensatory amplification |\n| L1 Gradient | Reward SGS | 0/2 | 36% | No-op (gradient ≠ causality) |\n| L2 Output | RAVE ceil=2.0 | 0/1 | 3% | EMA anchor pollution |\n| L2 Output | RAVE ceil=1.5 | 0/1 | 8% | EMA anchor pollution |\n| L3 Repr. | TCN σ=0.2 | 2/8 | 91%/45% | Dose insufficient in complex envs |\n| L3 Repr. | TCN σ=0.5 | 0/3 | 40% | Overdose/underdose |\n| L3 Repr. | TCN σ=1.0 | 1/1† | 27% | Learning destroyed by noise |\n| Control | Emb removal | 8/8 | 15-37% | (removes feature entirely) |\n\n## Experiment Coverage Context\n\nThe full experiment design (from review_context.yaml) includes these planned experiments:\n- Walker Walk: 008d multi-seed ✓, 008f multi-seed ✓, 008a multi-seed ✓, 008e_v3 (4 seeds) ✓, 015b frozen ✓\n- Cheetah Run: baseline (5 seeds) ✓, CIVO trigger (2 real + 1 predicted), causal control (2 real + 1 predicted), unexposed (2 real + 1 predicted)\n- Cartpole Swingup: baseline, CIVO trigger, causal control — ALL predicted\n- Hopper Hop: baseline, CIVO trigger, no dt_emb — ALL predicted\n- RSSM fidelity analysis ✓, Actor entropy ✓, V(s) vs MC ✓, Embedding cosine similarity ✓\n- NVE threshold analysis ✓\n- Intervention Hierarchy ✓ (mostly real data)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the experimental design rigorous? Are controls adequate? Is the NVE metric well-defined and justified? Is the 2×2 ablation matrix well-constructed?\n\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are seed counts sufficient? Is reporting of individual seeds (vs. aggregates) appropriate? How robust are claims given the variance?\n\n3. **Experimental completeness**: Cross-reference with the planned experiments above — what fraction is real vs. predicted? Is this disclosed transparently?\n\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters specified?\n\n5. **Claim-evidence alignment**: Does each claim have adequate experimental support? Any overclaiming?\n\n6. **Technical correctness**: Any methodological flaws, confounds, or alternative explanations not considered?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n\n### Summary\n2-3 sentences on what the paper does.\n\n### Strengths (S1-Sn)\nNumbered, specific.\n\n### Weaknesses (W1-Wn)\nNumbered, specific. For each, indicate severity: Minor / Moderate / Major.\n\n### Questions for Authors (Q1-Qn)\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n### Preliminary Score (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\nScore + 1-sentence justification.\n\n### Confidence (1-5: 1=low, 5=expert)\n","uuid":"8b97c9cd-dea4-4f32-863f-a80b948a68b9","session_id":"c17d4e9b-d260-4cdf-87d4-b48964bed707"}
