{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BegJ6iUurn8QS95VWchFhp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Conclusion}\n2\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n3\t\n4\tWe present a factorial diagnostic of temporal abstraction in DreamerV3, identifying trained $\\dt$-embedding with multi-rate data exposure as the causal factor behind CIVO ($\\nve \\geq 1.5\\times$, 27/29, AUROC${=}0.971$). The RSSM's $2.78\\times$ reward prediction gap drives critic overestimation through forward-pass mechanisms that no gradient-level intervention eliminates (SGS 0/3--1/6). TCN provides the only observed CIVO reduction but remains unreliable (2/3 Walker, 0/4 cross-environment); the effective Cheetah dose ($\\sigma{=}1.0$) destroys learning despite preventing CIVO. The feedback architecture is the structural factor: $\\dt$-conditioning succeeds with MPC re-planning but fails under imagination-based bootstrapping.\n5\t\n6\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n7\tOur analysis centers on Walker Walk with fewer cross-environment seeds; all environments use proprioceptive state, and generalization to pixel observations remains open. The NVE $\\geq 1.5\\times$ threshold is validated in-sample ($n{=}29$, two environments); held-out evaluation is needed before deployment as a general diagnostic. Generalization to transformer-based world models remains open.\n8\t\n9\t\\paragraph{Future directions.}\n10\tHierarchical temporal abstraction may avoid flat-conditioning failure by maintaining separate dynamics per scale. Code and logs will be released upon acceptance.\n11\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01G3NX7j17XMN1tmZ9ZwYabH","session_id":"c17d4e9b-d260-4cdf-87d4-b48964bed707","uuid":"dcfe2b4f-05a8-45d7-9631-99f7c8f7c878","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:41:19.043Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019pDTQJWBBBhqMk4KjwTCsq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Trcq7dLoRNgAf4SQWeJecK","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/docs/paper/sections/related_work.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8122,"cache_read_input_tokens":41718,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8122,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01G3NX7j17XMN1tmZ9ZwYabH","session_id":"c17d4e9b-d260-4cdf-87d4-b48964bed707","uuid":"6b360a03-d3c3-447e-8120-9abda0e930d3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Trcq7dLoRNgAf4SQWeJecK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Related Work}\n2\t\\label{sec:related_work}\n3\t\n4\t\\paragraph{Temporal abstraction in RL.}\n5\tThe options framework \\citep{sutton1999options}, option-critic \\citep{bacon2017optioncritc}, HAM \\citep{parr1998ham}, and MAXQ \\citep{dietterich2000maxq} formalize temporal abstraction through hierarchical policies. Semi-MDP theory \\citep{bradtke1995smdp} provides the mathematical foundation. Action-repetition methods \\citep{sharma2017figar, biedenkapp2021temporl} achieve simpler temporal abstraction via real transitions. The Dreamer family \\citep{hafner2020dreamerv1, hafner2021dreamerv2, hafner2023dreamerv3, hafner2025dreamerv4} trains policies in imagination at fixed $\\dt{=}1$; hierarchical variants \\citep{gumbsch2024thick, mattes2024hieros, li2025lsimagine} maintain separate dynamics per scale. TAWM \\citep{nhu2025tawm} conditions a world model on $\\dt$ within TD-MPC2 \\citep{hansen2024tdmpc2} and achieves consistent gains; the critical distinction is that MPC re-plans from real states, so model errors do not accumulate into a critic (\\S\\ref{sec:discussion}).\n6\t\n7\t\\paragraph{Value overestimation and model fidelity.}\n8\tMaximization bias \\citep{thrun1993issues} has been addressed by Double Q-learning \\citep{vanhasselt2016double}, TD3 \\citep{fujimoto2018td3}, and ensemble methods \\citep{chen2021redq}; in model-based settings, MBPO \\citep{janner2019mbpo} and MOPO \\citep{yu2020mopo} bound rollout error, while MOReL \\citep{kidambi2020morel} and COMBO \\citep{yu2021combo} penalize model uncertainty. \\citet{lu2018nondelusional} identify delusional bias from policy-class constraints. CIVO is mechanistically distinct: the amplifier is the world model's co-adaptation with a learned temporal embedding, producing systematic reward-prediction bias at $\\dt > 1$. Compounding error in multi-step rollouts \\citep{lambert2020objective, asadi2019combating} exacerbates this bias; decision-aware model learning \\citep{hamrick2021planning} optimizes for planning performance but does not address $\\dt$-conditioned co-adaptation. DreamSmooth \\citep{lee2024dreamsmooth} smooths reward prediction targets but does not address the fidelity gap at $\\dt > 1$. Our TCN intervention relates to training-with-noise \\citep{bishop1995noise} and NoisyNet \\citep{fortunato2018noisynet}; PETS \\citep{chua2018pets} and STEVE \\citep{buckman2018steve} use ensemble disagreement for epistemic uncertainty. TCN's environment-specific dose sensitivity (\\S\\ref{sec:forward_results}) suggests CIVO is a structural vulnerability, not a regularization deficiency. Our factorial diagnostic follows \\citet{henderson2018deep}.\n9\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01G3NX7j17XMN1tmZ9ZwYabH","session_id":"c17d4e9b-d260-4cdf-87d4-b48964bed707","uuid":"658a82fb-0241-45db-bb9b-78f3747fbf45","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:41:19.715Z"}
