{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01T9Jh2Ri6FPr4NSAA1MQieD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VHAUr8SnUzXQHhNMrSWgUz","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/docs/paper/sections/introduction.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21908,"cache_read_input_tokens":38659,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":21908,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WdHC5g4HMLVvVb1MARNHJr","session_id":"c17d4e9b-d260-4cdf-87d4-b48964bed707","uuid":"6673558a-a6a8-4759-a456-f5222b714a97"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VHAUr8SnUzXQHhNMrSWgUz","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Introduction}\n2\t\\label{sec:introduction}\n3\t\n4\tTAWM \\citep{nhu2025tawm} shows that conditioning a world model on step size $\\dt$ yields consistent gains within TD-MPC2's model-predictive control (MPC) framework \\citep{hansen2024tdmpc2}. Can the same $\\dt$-conditioning bring temporal abstraction to imagination-based actor-critic methods like DreamerV3 \\citep{hafner2023dreamerv3}, where policies are trained entirely in imagined rollouts?\n5\t\n6\tWe find that it cannot. Every variable-rate configuration degrades performance catastrophically (14--19\\% of baseline). Through a factorial analysis on Walker Walk \\citep{tassa2018dmcontrol}, we isolate the combination of a \\emph{trained} $\\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the causal trigger. This combination enables the RSSM reward head to develop a $2.78\\times$ prediction gap between $\\dt{=}1$ and $\\dt{=}2$, which the critic internalizes as optimistic value estimates, a failure mode we term \\emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO), analogous to delusional bias \\citep{lu2018nondelusional} but model-induced rather than policy-class-induced. A normalized value-to-eval (NVE) threshold of $\\geq 1.5\\times$ achieves 27/29 classification ($n{=}29$, two environments, AUROC${=}0.971$, 95\\% CI: 0.904--1.000).\n7\t\n8\tGradient decomposition reveals that gradient magnitude does not predict causal importance: the reward head contributes 38--49\\% of $\\nabla_\\theta e_{\\dt}$ yet reward-only stopping fails, while the observation decoder (16--22\\%) carries the critical signal. Full-pathway gradient stopping eliminates CIVO in 0/3 seeds, confirming forward-pass mechanisms dominate. A three-level intervention hierarchy spanning 9 configurations shows that no tested intervention reliably eliminates CIVO; TCN ($\\sigma{=}0.2$) provides the only observed reduction but remains unreliable (2/3 Walker Walk seeds, 0/4 cross-environment configs). These findings are directly relevant to robotics: as world-model RL moves toward real systems with variable-rate sensing and multi-timescale control, understanding when temporal conditioning fails---and why standard countermeasures cannot fix it---is essential for safe deployment.\n9\t\n10\t\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n11\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n12\t    \\item \\textbf{Failure mode identification.} We identify CIVO as specific to imagination-based actor-critic with temporal conditioning and isolate trained $\\dt$-embedding $+$ multi-rate exposure as jointly necessary through factorial experiments (6/6 seeds exceed NVE threshold, 5/6 collapse across two environments).\n13\t    \\item \\textbf{MPC vs.\\ imagination boundary.} We identify the feedback architecture as the structural factor: $\\dt$-conditioning succeeds in TD-MPC2's MPC re-planning but fails under imagination-based bootstrapping, where model errors compound into biased value targets with no real-data anchor.\n14\t    \\item \\textbf{Gradient magnitude $\\neq$ causal importance.} The reward head contributes the largest gradient share (38--49\\%) yet reward-only stopping fails; the observation decoder (16--22\\%) is causally critical. Obs-decoder SGS achieves only 1/6 prevention, confirming redundant gradient pathways.\n15\t    \\item \\textbf{Three-level intervention hierarchy.} Across 9 configurations at gradient, output, and representation levels, no intervention reliably eliminates CIVO. TCN ($\\sigma{=}0.2$) provides the only observed CIVO reduction but remains unreliable (2/3 Walker, 0/4 cross-environment), serving as a diagnostic contrast that identifies the representation level as the relevant intervention target.\n16\t    \\item \\textbf{Online detection.} A lightweight NVE-based detector achieves 100\\% sensitivity (5/5) with predictive warning in 2/5 cases, enabling early termination of compromised runs.\n17\t\\end{enumerate}\n18\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WdHC5g4HMLVvVb1MARNHJr","session_id":"c17d4e9b-d260-4cdf-87d4-b48964bed707","uuid":"beec4f7b-ef9f-4354-b5a8-cc19c00e1cfe","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:41:48.304Z"}
