{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01T9Jh2Ri6FPr4NSAA1MQieD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01S2gNyDGPhBSX3Tm7313scx","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/docs/paper/sections/abstract_body.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21908,"cache_read_input_tokens":38659,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":21908,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WdHC5g4HMLVvVb1MARNHJr","session_id":"c17d4e9b-d260-4cdf-87d4-b48964bed707","uuid":"def4baeb-89ee-4358-9467-6172db3e5f25"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S2gNyDGPhBSX3Tm7313scx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tTemporal abstraction via variable-rate imagination promises to extend the effective planning horizon of world-model reinforcement learning, and $\\Delta t$-conditioning has proven effective in model-predictive control (MPC) methods. We find that this approach cannot be directly transferred to imagination-based actor-critic training: every variable-rate configuration of DreamerV3 we test degrades performance. Through factorial diagnostics on two continuous-control tasks (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run), we identify the combination of trained temporal embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the causal factor and characterize the resulting failure mode: \\emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO). The trained embedding enables the reward head of the recurrent state-space model (RSSM) to develop a $2.78\\times$ prediction gap between $\\Delta t{=}1$ and $\\Delta t{=}2$, which the critic internalizes as optimistic values, universally degrading performance to below 30\\% of baseline across all six tested seeds in two environments ($n{=}6$). Gradient decomposition reveals that the observation decoder channel, not the reward head, carries the critical $\\Delta t$-specific spatial information that drives the fidelity gap. Removing multi-rate data exposure suppresses CIVO entirely (5 seeds, all below the $1.5\\times$ diagnostic threshold); full-pathway gradient stopping reduces CIVO severity in one of two Walker seeds (peak NVE $4.12\\times$ to $1.66\\times$) but eliminates it in neither (0/3 across environments), exposing mechanisms beyond gradient-mediated co-adaptation. A lightweight online detector identifies all five CIVO cases (100\\% sensitivity) with advance warning before performance degradation in two cases, enabling early intervention without a reference baseline. The contrast with MPC methods identifies the feedback architecture (imagination-based bootstrapping versus re-planning) as the structural factor that transforms manageable model imprecision into pathological value dynamics. Targeted gradient stopping on the identified obs-decoder pathway fails to prevent CIVO (1/6 seeds across two environments), confirming that CIVO operates through redundant gradient channels that no single-pathway intervention addresses. A three-level intervention hierarchy (gradient, output, representation) reveals that no tested intervention reliably eliminates CIVO. Representation-level noise (TCN) provides the only observed reduction in CIVO severity among tested interventions, but remains unreliable even under optimal conditions (2/3 Walker Walk seeds, 0/4 cross-environment configs), serving primarily as a diagnostic contrast that identifies the representation level as the relevant intervention target rather than a viable mitigation.\n2\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WdHC5g4HMLVvVb1MARNHJr","session_id":"c17d4e9b-d260-4cdf-87d4-b48964bed707","uuid":"5f277638-6173-453f-8196-92af6dd1a827","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:41:48.547Z"}
