{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TG5tsw3HMQ4u5duJfygNqx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016adQBHVmfG3zuarPC59B3Q","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/docs/paper/sections/analysis.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2884,"cache_read_input_tokens":60567,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2884,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":68,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WdHC5g4HMLVvVb1MARNHJr","session_id":"c17d4e9b-d260-4cdf-87d4-b48964bed707","uuid":"5d3259ed-994d-429a-9dec-d5898ed2d656"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016adQBHVmfG3zuarPC59B3Q","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Discussion}\n2\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n3\t\n4\tFull mechanistic analysis, gradient decomposition, and seed-divergence discussion are in Appendix~\\ref{sec:appendix_analysis}.\n5\t\n6\t\\paragraph{CIVO mechanism and gradient analysis.}\n7\tThe RSSM develops a $2.78\\times$ reward prediction gap under trained conditioning (Table~\\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}), creating a model-induced feedback loop analogous to delusional bias \\citep{lu2018nondelusional}. Gradient decomposition (Figure~\\ref{fig:gradient_decomp}) shows the reward head contributes 38--49\\% of $\\nabla_\\theta e_{\\dt}$ yet reward-only SGS fails (NVE $10.42\\times$), while the observation decoder (16--22\\%) carries the critical signal. All SGS variants exhibit compensatory amplification; full-pathway SGS (0/3) establishes forward-pass conditioning as the irreducible vulnerability floor (Appendix~\\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).\n8\t\n9\t\\paragraph{Why not standard overestimation countermeasures?}\n10\tDouble Q-learning \\citep{vanhasselt2016double}, TD3 \\citep{fujimoto2018td3}, and ensemble critics \\citep{chen2021redq} target \\emph{estimation error}---the upward bias from maximizing over noisy Q-approximations during policy improvement. CIVO is mechanistically upstream: the RSSM's $2.78\\times$ reward fidelity gap at $\\dt > 1$ biases the critic's \\emph{inputs}, not its training dynamics. An ensemble of critics would converge to the same wrong value because they share biased imagination data---the distinction between measurement bias (CIVO) and measurement noise (standard overestimation). MBPO-style rollout truncation \\citep{janner2019mbpo} mixes real and model data, but DreamerV3 trains entirely within imagination. MOReL \\citep{kidambi2020morel} and COMBO \\citep{yu2021combo} penalize model uncertainty, but the RSSM is \\emph{confidently wrong} at $\\dt > 1$, producing low-variance biased predictions that uncertainty penalties would not flag.\n11\t\n12\t\\paragraph{MPC vs.\\ imagination-based actor-critic.}\n13\tTAWM \\citep{nhu2025tawm} succeeds with $\\dt$-conditioning in TD-MPC2 \\citep{hansen2024tdmpc2} because MPC re-plans from real states at every step, so model errors do not accumulate into a critic. In DreamerV3, the actor-critic trains entirely within imagination: the RSSM's fidelity gap at $\\dt > 1$ produces biased value estimates that the actor exploits, closing a positive feedback loop with no MPC counterpart (Appendix~\\ref{sec:mpc_immune}).\n14\t\n15\t\\paragraph{Practitioner guidelines.}\n16\tThe intervention hierarchy (Table~\\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}) yields actionable recommendations: (1)~monitor for CIVO using the NVE diagnostic ($\\geq 1.5\\times$, AUROC${=}0.971$, $n{=}29$; Appendix~\\ref{sec:roc_analysis}); (2)~no tested intervention reliably eliminates CIVO---TCN ($\\sigma{=}0.2$) provides the only observed reduction but remains unreliable (2/3 Walker, 0/4 cross-environment), with the effective Cheetah dose destroying learning (eval 27\\%); (3)~avoid gradient-level interventions (compensatory amplification) and output-level value capping (anchor pollution).\n17\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WdHC5g4HMLVvVb1MARNHJr","session_id":"c17d4e9b-d260-4cdf-87d4-b48964bed707","uuid":"ae46aa40-df27-45ec-b0d9-3335ad9e2ec6","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:41:54.194Z"}
